release-signers: the OpenPGP public keys whose signed release tags kuwai trusts.

What goes here
  One armored public key per file, named NAME.asc. Nothing else is read.
  kuwai-setup, kuwai update-self and root's own check build a keyring from these
  files alone (in a temporary GNUPGHOME; root's from its own copy's folder), never from
  your own GnuPG keyring, so a key you happen to trust elsewhere signs nothing here.
  While this folder holds no key, every one of them refuses: no release can be checked.

Adding the release key (once, by the maintainer)
  gpg --list-secret-keys --keyid-format long          find the key's fingerprint (40 hex digits)
  gpg --export --armor FINGERPRINT > release-signers/kuwai-release.asc
  gpg --show-keys --with-fingerprint release-signers/kuwai-release.asc
                                                      check the fingerprint it prints is yours
  git add release-signers/kuwai-release.asc && git commit -m "Release key"
  Publish the fingerprint where people can check it (README, website, keyserver).

Tagging a release
  kuwai release vX.Y.Z
  On main, clean. It runs, with your key from this folder:
    d=$(bin/kuwai-verify manifest-rev . HEAD)
    git -c gpg.format=openpgp tag -s -u FINGERPRINT -m "kuwai X.Y.Z" -m "kuwai-tree-sha256 $d" vX.Y.Z HEAD
  then checks the tag the way an install will, and deletes it if that fails.
  The kuwai-tree-sha256 line is the digest of every file in the tree (path,
  executable bit, content, link target): root checks its copy against it without
  git. A tag made by plain `git tag -s` has no such line and is refused.
  Publish it: git push origin vX.Y.Z (a release tag never moves once pushed).

Changing keys
  Root keeps one list: /usr/local/lib/kuwai/signers/fingerprints. The first
  install fills it from the fingerprints kuwai-setup names (this folder's keys).
  After that kuwai-setup names none, and each release root takes replaces the
  list with the keys that release lists here, but only once the release's tag
  checked out against a key already on the list. So a key changes in two releases:

    1. Add the new key's .asc here next to the old one; tag that release with
       the OLD key. Root takes it (signed by a key it trusts) and now trusts both.
    2. Remove the old key's .asc; tag the next release with the NEW key. Root
       takes it (the new key is trusted since step 1) and drops the old one.

  A release must list the key that signs it (kuwai release refuses otherwise).
  From then on a tag signed by the removed key is refused, and naming it to
  root again (--signer) is refused too. A release older than root's (only with
  --allow-older) can drop keys from the list but never add one.
  Each machine moves only by installing the releases in between: a machine
  that skipped step 1 refuses step 2's release until it has taken step 1's.
  The list is written in one step (a new file renamed over the old), before
  root's current release changes.
